1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,638
On the evening of the eighth of May 2026,

2
00:00:03,638 --> 00:00:10,287
people along the Odisha coast watched something climb out of the sky above Dr APJ Abdul Kalam Island.

3
00:00:10,287 --> 00:00:11,525
And then: nothing.

4
00:00:11,525 --> 00:00:12,634
No announcement.

5
00:00:12,634 --> 00:00:20,821
No statement from the government, or from DRDO. For a full day, Indian media called it the mystery missile, and guessed.

6
00:00:24,367 --> 00:00:29,585
When the Defence Ministry finally posted, it confirmed an advanced Agni missile carrying MIRV,

7
00:00:29,585 --> 00:00:35,653
and said its payloads had been aimed at different points spread across a large area of the Indian Ocean.

8
00:00:39,367 --> 00:00:44,185
Let's do the simple version first, because the acronym is worse than the idea.

9
00:00:44,185 --> 00:00:48,333
MIRV stands for Multiple Independently Targetable Re-entry Vehicle.

10
00:00:48,333 --> 00:00:52,420
Forget all of that except three words: many, independent, targets.

11
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:56,141
A normal ballistic missile carries one warhead to one place.

12
00:00:56,141 --> 00:01:01,016
A MIRVed missile carries several, and each one can go somewhere different.

13
00:01:05,067 --> 00:01:06,329
Here's the picture.

14
00:01:06,329 --> 00:01:08,979
The missile climbs out of the atmosphere.

15
00:01:08,979 --> 00:01:13,647
At the top, the nose opens and releases a platform carrying the warheads.

16
00:01:13,647 --> 00:01:19,074
That platform steers, lets one warhead go, shifts position, and lets the next one go.

17
00:01:19,074 --> 00:01:22,150
Each warhead then falls along its own path.

18
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:29,910
And engineers have a name for that platform.

19
00:01:29,910 --> 00:01:31,283
They call it the bus.

20
00:01:31,283 --> 00:01:35,653
That isn't a nickname I've invented for you — it's the standard term.

21
00:01:35,653 --> 00:01:41,959
Because that is exactly what it does: it carries passengers, and drops each one at a different stop.

22
00:01:41,959 --> 00:01:45,131
That's MIRV. Everything after this is detail.

23
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:50,305
Four questions, then.

24
00:01:50,305 --> 00:01:54,838
How did India build a missile like this, starting from almost nothing?

25
00:01:54,838 --> 00:01:56,434
What is Agni-5, exactly?

26
00:01:56,434 --> 00:02:02,245
Why would a country that promises never to strike first need many warheads on one missile?

27
00:02:02,245 --> 00:02:04,397
And where does this go next?

28
00:02:06,467 --> 00:02:07,477
Start in 1983.

29
00:02:07,477 --> 00:02:10,035
India has almost no missile industry.

30
00:02:10,035 --> 00:02:14,950
The government launches the Integrated Guided Missile Development Progra

31
00:02:14,950 --> 00:02:19,865
mme and puts a rocket engineer named A.P.J. Abdul Kalam in charge of it.

32
00:02:19,865 --> 00:02:21,885
Five missiles, one programme.

33
00:02:21,885 --> 00:02:25,319
Prithvi, a short-range surface-to-surface missile.

34
00:02:25,319 --> 00:02:28,851
Trishul and Akash, two surface-to-air missiles.

35
00:02:36,567 --> 00:02:38,263
Nag, an anti-tank missile.

36
00:02:38,263 --> 00:02:42,785
And Agni, the ballistic one, which is the family this episode is about.

37
00:02:42,785 --> 00:02:43,853
Agni means fire.

38
00:02:43,853 --> 00:02:46,617
And the first Agni was not a weapon at all.

39
00:02:46,617 --> 00:02:52,271
It was a technology demonstrator, flown from Chandipur in 1989, built to prove one thing:

40
00:02:52,271 --> 00:02:58,161
that India could bring something back down through the atmosphere without it burning up.

41
00:03:05,067 --> 00:03:09,661
That problem, re-entry, is the hard part of a long-range ballistic missile.

42
00:03:09,661 --> 00:03:13,530
It is also why the warhead on one is called a re-entry vehicle.

43
00:03:13,530 --> 00:03:18,487
The programme itself was wound up in 2008, once India had what it needed from it.

44
00:03:18,487 --> 00:03:21,205
But Agni kept going, and kept climbing.

45
00:03:25,767 --> 00:03:30,081
Climbing is the right word, because the Agni story is a ladder.

46
00:03:30,081 --> 00:03:34,732
Agni-1, reaching roughly seven hundred to twelve hundred kilometres.

47
00:03:34,732 --> 00:03:37,563
Agni-2, two to three and a half thousand.

48
00:03:37,563 --> 00:03:39,720
Agni-3, three to five thousand.

49
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:44,438
Agni-4, four thousand kilometres, but far lighter and easier to move.

50
00:03:44,438 --> 00:03:48,176
And then, on the nineteenth of April 2012, Agni-5.

51
00:03:50,233 --> 00:03:56,238
Each rung took years, because every extra thousand kilometres is a harder problem than the one before.

52
00:03:56,238 --> 00:04:03,291
You need more stages, lighter materials, better guidance, and a re-entry vehicle that survives a longer and hotter fall.

53
00:04:03,291 --> 00:04:05,690
So why does range matter this much?

54
00:04:14,533 --> 00:04:18,660
Because a missile that can only reach your neighbour can only deter your neighbour.

55
00:04:18,660 --> 00:04:20,871
Reach decides who has to take you seriously.

56
00:04:20,871 --> 00:04:24,408
Agni-5 is the rung where that ladder reaches intercontinental distance,

57
00:04:24,408 --> 00:04:29,179
and where India joined a very small group of countries that can build such a thing at all.

58
00:04:31,267 --> 00:04:32,151
So what is it?

59
00:04:32,151 --> 00:04:35,392
Agni-5 is a three-stage missile running on solid fuel,

60
00:04:35,392 --> 00:04:40,931
about seventeen and a half metres long, and weighing somewhere around fifty tonnes at launch.

61
00:04:40,931 --> 00:04:45,469
The government describes its range as in excess of five thousand kilometres.

62
00:04:45,469 --> 00:04:49,064
Outside analysts estimate considerably more; some say eight.

63
00:04:49,064 --> 00:04:53,254
The exact figure is classified, and India has never published it.

64
00:04:58,267 --> 00:05:00,807
And how far is five thousand kilometres?

65
00:05:00,807 --> 00:05:03,038
Put it in time instead of distance.

66
00:05:03,038 --> 00:05:06,322
A passenger jet needs about six hours to cover that.

67
00:05:06,322 --> 00:05:08,800
Agni-5 does it in under twenty minutes.

68
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:16,235
Like Pralay in our first episode, it is canisterised: sealed in a tube, carried on a transporter, able to move by road.

69
00:05:16,235 --> 00:05:21,678
For a strategic missile that matters even more than it does for a battlefield one.

70
00:05:25,967 --> 00:05:29,832
A missile sitting in a silo whose location is known can be targeted.

71
00:05:29,832 --> 00:05:37,395
A missile that moves, and that can be fired from wherever it happens to be standing, is far harder to destroy before it ever launches.

72
00:05:37,395 --> 00:05:42,176
Survivability is the entire point, and in a few minutes you'll see exactly why.

73
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:48,883
Now, the MIRV itself.

74
00:05:48,883 --> 00:05:54,950
On the eleventh of March 2024, DRDO flew Agni-5 carrying multiple re-entry vehicles for the first time.

75
00:05:54,950 --> 00:05:56,934
The mission was called Divyastra.

76
00:05:56,934 --> 00:06:05,626
It went up from Kalam Island, and the official release said that telemetry and radar stations tracked multiple re-entry vehicles through the flight,

77
00:06:05,626 --> 00:06:08,842
and that the mission met its designed parameters.

78
00:06:10,933 --> 00:06:14,195
The Prime Minister said he was proud of the scientists.

79
00:06:14,195 --> 00:06:17,398
The Defence Minister called it an exceptional success.

80
00:06:17,398 --> 00:06:20,950
Then came the eighth of May 2026, the launch we opened with.

81
00:06:20,950 --> 00:06:24,794
The Ministry of Defence confirmed an advanced Agni carrying MIRV,

82
00:06:24,794 --> 00:06:32,781
and said the payloads had been aimed at different targets spatially distributed over a large geographical area of the Indian Ocean.

83
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,018
Now notice what it did not say.

84
00:06:37,018 --> 00:06:38,847
It did not name the variant.

85
00:06:38,847 --> 00:06:40,423
It did not give a range.

86
00:06:40,423 --> 00:06:42,567
It did not say how many warheads.

87
00:06:42,567 --> 00:06:46,666
Analysts estimated, and some suggested this was an early Agni-6.

88
00:06:46,666 --> 00:06:52,011
Those are inferences, not announcements, and it is worth keeping the two apart.

89
00:06:55,867 --> 00:06:57,778
So why go to all this trouble?

90
00:06:57,778 --> 00:06:59,258
One word: interception.

91
00:06:59,258 --> 00:07:04,191
A missile-defence system works by firing an interceptor at an incoming warhead.

92
00:07:04,191 --> 00:07:07,335
Against one warhead, that is one problem to solve.

93
00:07:07,335 --> 00:07:11,405
Against a missile that splits into several, each on its own path,

94
00:07:11,405 --> 00:07:16,761
the defender has to solve it several times over, at once, in a matter of minutes.

95
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:24,240
MIRV is, above everything else, an answer to missile defence.

96
00:07:24,240 --> 00:07:31,486
It is a way of making sure that if you ever did have to retaliate, enough of that retaliation would actually arrive.

97
00:07:31,486 --> 00:07:32,724
So who can do this?

98
00:07:32,724 --> 00:07:37,050
The United States, Russia, China, the United Kingdom and France.

99
00:07:39,133 --> 00:07:47,004
Pakistan says its Ababeel missile has the capability; independent verification is limited, and some analysts doubt how mature it is.

100
00:07:47,004 --> 00:07:52,393
India's two tests put it in a group that until very recently was five countries long.

101
00:07:56,900 --> 00:08:00,090
And here is the part that makes all of this make sense,

102
00:08:00,090 --> 00:08:05,103
because a weapon only means something next to the rules its owner has set for using it.

103
00:08:05,103 --> 00:08:11,938
On the fourth of January 2003, India's Cabinet Committee on Security published the main points of its nuclear doctrine.

104
00:08:11,938 --> 00:08:13,476
Three of them matter here.

105
00:08:13,476 --> 00:08:15,413
One: credible minimum deterrence.

106
00:08:15,413 --> 00:08:20,042
India aims to hold enough to deter, rather than to match anybody's numbers.

107
00:08:27,300 --> 00:08:28,441
Two: no first use.

108
00:08:28,441 --> 00:08:31,442
India says it will not use nuclear weapons first,

109
00:08:31,442 --> 00:08:38,046
and that they would be used only in retaliation for a nuclear attack on Indian territory or on Indian forces.

110
00:08:38,046 --> 00:08:45,671
Three: if that retaliation ever came, the doctrine says it would be massive, and designed to inflict unacceptable damage.

111
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,491
The same announcement set out who decides.

112
00:08:54,491 --> 00:08:57,271
The Nuclear Command Authority has two councils.

113
00:08:57,271 --> 00:09:04,106
The Political Council, chaired by the Prime Minister, is the only body that can authorise the use of nuclear weapons.

114
00:09:04,106 --> 00:09:10,661
The Executive Council, chaired by the National Security Adviser, advises it and carries out its directions.

115
00:09:19,200 --> 00:09:24,614
And the same meeting approved the appointment of a Commander-in-Chief of the Strategic Forces Command,

116
00:09:24,614 --> 00:09:26,875
to manage all of India's strategic forces.

117
00:09:26,875 --> 00:09:30,081
That command holds the weapons and carries out those orders,

118
00:09:30,081 --> 00:09:35,375
and it is led by a three-star officer drawn in turn from the Army, the Navy and the Air Force.

119
00:09:41,267 --> 00:09:43,284
Now put MIRV back into that frame.

120
00:09:43,284 --> 00:09:48,933
If you have promised never to strike first, then everything rests on being able to strike second:

121
00:09:48,933 --> 00:09:51,815
after absorbing an attack, with whatever is left.

122
00:09:51,815 --> 00:09:53,774
Mobility keeps the missile alive.

123
00:09:53,774 --> 00:09:55,619
MIRV keeps the answer credible.

124
00:09:55,619 --> 00:10:01,798
That is the logic Agni-5 is built for, and without the doctrine the hardware makes very little sense.

125
00:10:06,933 --> 00:10:10,860
Not everyone reads it the same way, and both sides are worth hearing.

126
00:10:10,860 --> 00:10:18,488
The case for: a no-first-use country needs its retaliation to be survivable and credible, and mobility plus MIRV delivers exactly that.

127
00:10:18,488 --> 00:10:24,490
The case for caution: analysts point out that a MIRVed missile concentrates many warheads on one launcher,

128
00:10:24,490 --> 00:10:31,465
which makes each launcher a bigger prize, and that capability of this kind tends to push neighbours to answer in kind.

129
00:10:38,300 --> 00:10:47,968
Writing for the Manohar Parrikar Institute for Defence Studies and Analyses in May 2026, Ajey Lele argued that the test read as a restrained signal rather than a provocation,

130
00:10:47,968 --> 00:10:54,455
and that a much longer-range test could have been taken as escalatory by powers well beyond China and Pakistan.

131
00:10:59,333 --> 00:11:02,165
Both of those things can be true at the same time.

132
00:11:02,165 --> 00:11:09,182
Deterrence is an argument about what other people will believe, which is exactly why serious analysts disagree about it.

133
00:11:11,400 --> 00:11:12,286
What's coming?

134
00:11:12,286 --> 00:11:18,073
Agni-Prime, a lighter two-stage missile in the one-to-two-thousand-kilometre class, is in trials.

135
00:11:18,073 --> 00:11:20,671
Agni-6 is reported to be under development,

136
00:11:20,671 --> 00:11:26,931
with ranges quoted anywhere between eight and twelve thousand kilometres, and notice that word, reported.

137
00:11:26,931 --> 00:11:30,533
There has been no official announcement of an Agni-6 at all.

138
00:11:30,533 --> 00:11:37,206
Lele's argument is that India may not need one urgently, since Agni-5 already reaches intercontinental distance,

139
00:11:37,206 --> 00:11:42,171
and that a longer test would buy more diplomatic trouble than deterrent value.

140
00:11:45,200 --> 00:11:49,346
Meanwhile the MIRV version of Agni-5 is still in flight trials.

141
00:11:49,346 --> 00:11:52,586
The single-warhead version is the one in service.

142
00:11:52,586 --> 00:11:57,162
As of the fifteenth of November 2026, that is where things stand.

143
00:11:59,233 --> 00:12:02,130
If you remember one thing, make it three words.

144
00:12:02,130 --> 00:12:08,829
Far: officially beyond five thousand kilometres, with outside estimates higher and the real figure classified.

145
00:12:08,829 --> 00:12:12,631
Many: one missile, several warheads, each with its own target.

146
00:12:12,631 --> 00:12:16,674
And second: this is a weapon built to be used second, never first.

147
00:12:16,674 --> 00:12:21,320
That is the doctrine, and it is the reason the other two matter at all.

148
00:12:30,333 --> 00:12:37,559
Agni-5 started as a question about whether anything India built could survive coming back down through the atmosphere,

149
00:12:37,559 --> 00:12:41,627
tested off a range in 1989 by a programme that barely existed yet.

150
00:12:41,627 --> 00:12:49,277
Next on India's Defence, Decoded: the other half of this story, missile defence, and the shield India is building for itself.

151
00:12:49,277 --> 00:12:53,038
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

